      "side": 1,
      "message": "```\nI\u0027m not entirely happy with this because *this* code is correct, so changing it in this way is breaking it to avoid fixing something else.\n```\n\nYes, this is partially true.\n\nThis code is correct, but not flexible enough that\u0027s why we need to have 3 ways to find an inode:\n\n1. Find by nodeid\n2. Find by backing_inode\n3. Find by both nodeid and backing_inode\n\nThe third options isn\u0027t always correct because sometimes we may have backing_inode but don\u0027t have nodeid, so we wouldn\u0027t be able to use 3 option.\n\nIn this CL I\u0027m introducing 1 and 2 instead of 3 that we have but not actually use (please take a look on updated CL).\n\nSo basically we have 2 totally separate problems:\n\n1. We can\u0027t use nodeid !\u003d 0 for inodes with backing_inode (critical -- affecting current scenarios and blocking future work) -- can be easily fixed\n2. We aren\u0027t covering case where backing_inode is the same but nodeid is different (normal -- useful for the future cases) -- will take us much more time.\n\nSeams like correctly fixing critical problem firstly makes much more sense. So I can introduce test which check that nodeid is is can be not 0 with backing_inode.\n\nBut yes, I think it would be great if you can introduce support of one backing_inode for different nodeids at the same time.\n\nWhat do you think about it?",
